WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.969
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.969 --> 00:00:11.219
This incredible paper promises procedural
and physically-based rendering of glittering

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:12.360
surfaces.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:13.500
Whoa.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:19.620
Okay, I am sold… provided that it does as
well as advertised.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.860
We shall see about that!

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:26.930
Oh, goodness, the results look lovely.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:32.110
And now, while we marvel at these, let’s
discuss what these terms really mean.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:38.100
One, physically-based means that it is built
on a foundation that is based in physics.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.380
That is not surprising, as the name says so.

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:47.040
The surprise usually comes when people use
the term without careful consideration.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.430
You see, light transport researchers take
this term very seriously, if you claim that

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:55.640
your model is physically-based, you better
bring your A-game.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.090
We will inspect that.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:04.699
Two, the procedural part means that we ourselves
can algorithmically generate many of these

00:01:04.699 --> 00:01:07.090
materials models ourselves.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:12.520
For instance, this earlier paper was able
procedurally generate the geometry of climbing

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.909
plants and simulate their growth.

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:21.369
Or, procedural generation can come to the
rescue when we are creating a virtual environment

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:27.730
and we need a hundreds of different flowers,
thousands of blades of grass, and more.

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:33.620
This is an actual example of procedural geometry
that was created with the wonderful Terragen

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:34.700
program.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:40.500
Three, rendering means a computer program
that we run on our machine at home and it

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.040
creates a beautiful series of images, like
these ones.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:49.650
In the case of photorealistic rendering, we
typically need to wait from minutes to hours

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.759
for every single image.

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:55.580
So, how fast is this technique?

00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:00.799
Well, hold on to your papers, because we don’t
need to wait from minutes to hours for every

00:02:00.799 --> 00:02:01.799
image.

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:06.799
Instead, we need only 2.5 milliseconds per
frame.

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:08.940
Absolute witchcraft.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:14.180
The fact that we can do this in real time
blows my mind, and, yes!

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:19.739
This means that we can test the procedural
part in an interactive demo too!

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:24.220
Here we can play with a set of parameters,
look, the roughness of the surface is not

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:30.670
set in stone, we can specify it and see as
the material changes in real time.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:36.440
We can also change the density of microfacets,
these are tiny imperfections in the surface

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.870
that really make these materials come alive.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:46.610
And if we make the microfacet density much
larger, the material becomes more diffuse.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:50.670
And if we make them really small…ohoho!

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:51.989
Loving this.

00:02:51.989 --> 00:02:57.800
So, as much as I love this, I would also love
to know how accurate this is.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:03.379
For reference, here is a result from a previous
technique that is really accurate, however,

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:09.099
this is one of those methods that takes from
minutes to hours for just one image.

00:03:09.099 --> 00:03:13.660
And here is the other end of the spectrum,
this is a different technique that is lower

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:20.120
in quality, however, in return, it can produce
these in real time.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:23.239
So which one is the new one closer to?

00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:30.600
The accurate, slow one, or less accurate,
quick one What?!

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:37.290
Its quality is as good as the accurate one,
and it also runs in real time.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.500
The best of both worlds.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:40.500
Wow!

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.750
Now, of course, not even this technique is
perfect, the fact that this particular example

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:50.160
worked so well is great, but it doesn’t
always come out so well.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:55.730
And, I know, I know, you’re asking, can
we try this new method.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:04:01.370
And the answer is a resounding yes, you can
try it right now in multiple places!

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:07.910
There is a web-demo, and it was even implemented
in Shadertoy.

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:13.430
So, now we know what it means to render procedural
and physically-based rendering of glittering

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:16.420
surfaces in real time.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:17.900
Absolutely incredible.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:19.840
What a time to be alive!

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.949
And if you enjoyed this episode, we may have
two more incredible glint rendering papers

00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:27.960
coming up in the near future.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:31.099
This, and this.

00:04:31.099 --> 00:04:34.469
Let me know in the comments below if you would
like to see them.

00:04:34.469 --> 00:05:15.830
Write something like, “yes please!”.

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:19.909
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

